{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 21, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a nobleman and a leader of the Crusader state of Tripoli. He was born in 1140 and was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. *Early Life* Raymond III succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli at a young age after his father was assassinated in 1152. His mother Hodierna acted as regent until he came of age. Raymond III was taken captive by Nur ad-Din Zangi in 1164 during the Battle of Harim and was held prisoner for nearly a decade until he was released in 1173 after a ransom was paid.\n\n*Reign* After his release Raymond III returned to Tripoli and began to consolidate his power. He married Eschiva of Bures a wealthy heiress and widow of Walter of Saint Omer the Prince of Galilee. This marriage brought him significant wealth and influence. Raymond III was a skilled military leader and played a key role in several battles against the Zengid and Ayyubid dynasties. He was also a shrewd politician and formed alliances with other Crusader leaders and the Byzantine Empire.\n\n*Regency in Jerusalem* In 1174 Raymond III was appointed regent for King Baldwin IV of Jerusalem who was suffering from leprosy. Raymond III played a crucial role in the governance of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and helped to secure the succession of Baldwin V. However his regency was marked by controversy and power struggles with other factions in the kingdom. Raymond III was a member of the faction that opposed the influence of the Latin Patriarch of Jerusalem and the Knights Templar. He was also a rival of Guy of Lusignan who would eventually become King of Jerusalem. Raymond III's regency ended when Baldwin IV came of age and he returned to Tripoli. He died in 1187 without an heir and was succeeded by his godson Raymond IV Count of Tripoli who was the son of Bohemond III of Antioch.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray",